from ../plugins import Plugins

export anchor Migration:
    description:
        The migrate plugin, bundled with the compiler, notices projects
        written for an older Piton and offers to bring them up to date. It is
        a plugin, described in @{Plugins}, that inspects every project before
        a command runs, and adds `piton migrate`.

        Its source is in the compiler's repository at plugins/migrate: its
        declaration in plugin.pi and its code in src. It has no Piton files
        of its own.

    migrations: @{FrameworksToPlugins}
    command: @{MigrateCommand}

    capabilities:
        It may read files in the project, and propose edits to
        piton.config.pi and the project's .pi files, which are the only
        files a migration changes. It runs no programs and writes nothing
        itself.

    adding:
        A migration recognises one old way of writing a project, from the
        project's files as they are, without compiling them, and gives the
        whole new contents of each file it changes. When Piton changes in a
        way that breaks existing projects, the change comes with a migration.

export anchor FrameworksToPlugins:
    description:
        A configuration that lists its frameworks under frameworks, which is
        now an error, lists them under plugins instead: a framework is a
        plugin.

    detects:
        A key named frameworks in the piton-config anchor of piton.config.pi.

    edit:
        When the configuration has no plugins key, the frameworks key is
        renamed plugins, and its items stay as they are. When it has both,
        the frameworks items move to the end of the plugins list, and the
        frameworks key goes. Either key can hold its items as a block or as
        an inline list; an inline plugins list becomes a block, so the moved
        items can follow its own. Every configuration that has a frameworks
        key gets this edit, so none is left to change by hand.

export anchor MigrateCommand:
    commandName: migrate

    description:
        Applies every migration the project needs, without asking, since
        running it is the asking, and reports each file changed. A project
        that needs none is said to be up to date.

    namedArguments:
        check: Change nothing, and fail when the project needs a migration, naming each one, for CI.

    exitCode:
        success: 0
        outOfDate: 1, with --check, when a migration is needed
